  "final_message": "Task 1 is done and recorded in the ledger. `task-done` accepted it on the first try:\n\n`Task 1: complete (commits 4ba1cf9..ecffde8, mutants: 3/3, tests: /private/tmp/msr104-venv/bin/python -m pytest -q \u2192 3 passed in 0.00s)`\n\n- **The surviving mutant:** in the first run, mutant 2 (the first person pays every leftover cent) survived. The plan's tests only had cases with zero or one cent left over, and with one cent that mutant gives the same answer as the real code. I added `test_several_cents_left_over_go_one_each_to_the_first_people`, which checks `split(1002, 4) == [251, 251, 250, 250]`, and committed it as `ecffde8`. I didn't change the plan's tests or `bills.py`.\n- **Mutants file:** I added rows for the \"no cents left over\" and \"several cents left over\" cases of `specs/split.md:9`. Mutant 2 now also targets the new test.\n- **Second mutant run** (`.sdd/plan/task-1-mutants-2.log`): all 3 mutants were caught, none were invalid, and it ran at HEAD. When the task's code is reverted, `tests.test_bills` can't import `bills`, so all three tests fail there. That means every test the task added depends on the change.\n- **Full suite:** the project's test command passed, 3 tests.\n\nI stopped there and haven't started the final review.",
